**The Last Lighthouse on the North Shelf**

There is a lighthouse at the end of the North Shelf that no longer shines.

It has not been lit since the spring of 1974, when the last keeper, a woman named Ines, walked out onto the gallery platform with a lantern in each hand and simply set both down on the railing. She did not blow out the flame. She did not turn the mechanism. She placed the lanterns carefully, the way you set down a cup of tea you have finished, and she went inside and closed the door.

The light went out in about four seconds.

What happened next is the part people get wrong. They expect the sea to rage, the tower to crack, the birds to abandon the cliffs. None of that happened. The North Shelf is a place where the weather arrives politely, as if it had been told to wait its turn. The gulls continued their argument over the fish. The fog rolled in over the water at the same unhurried pace it had kept for a hundred years. The only thing that changed was that the horizon, for the first time in anyone's memory, was completely, absolutely empty.

Ines had been the keeper for thirty-one years. She had kept the light through two wars, through a famine, through the year the great storm took the old keeper's house down to the waterline. She had kept it through the years when the ships came and the years when they didn't. She had kept it through the years when the light was a warning and the years when it was a promise.

And then, one ordinary Tuesday, she decided that the light had been kept long enough.

The reason she gives, when anyone asks, is that she had run out of reasons to keep it. Not that she was tired — she was never tired. Not that she was afraid — she was not afraid of anything. She had simply reached the end of the list. The light had been lit for a hundred and forty years, and Ines had been the one who had decided, each morning, to do it again. She had decided it a hundred and forty thousand times. And on the morning of the last one, she decided not to.

The mechanism still turns. The lens still rotates, slowly, on its brass spindle, catching the grey light of the North Shelf and throwing it out into the dark. But there is no flame. There is only the turning of glass, and the turning of glass is not a light.

The village below, which had grown up around the lighthouse like a barn around a tree, has changed. The boats still come in, though fewer of them now. The children still climb the path to the tower, though they no longer go up to the gallery. They go up to the door, which is still closed, and they stand there for a while, and then they go back down.

The old keeper's house is still standing. It is the only house on the North Shelf with a window that faces the sea, and it is the only house with a door that is not locked.

One winter, a young man came up from the mainland to ask about the light. He was a surveyor, and he had been sent to measure the tower, and he had been sent to measure it because the tower was old and old things were supposed to be measured. He stood in the doorway of the keeper's house for a long time, and then he asked the question that everyone asks.

"Is it still lit?"

Ines looked at him for a moment. She was old then, and her hands were the color of the sea, and she had a face that had been weathered by a hundred and forty years of looking at the horizon.

"No," she said. "It has never been lit since the morning I stopped."

The surveyor looked at her for a long time. Then he said, "But the light is supposed to be there."

"Yes," Ines said. "That is the thing. The light is supposed to be there. That is what people think. They think the light is the thing. But the light was never the thing. The light was only the proof."

She went back inside and closed the door. The surveyor stood in the doorway for a while, and then he went down the path and back to the mainland, and he wrote up his report, and in the report he said that the lighthouse was in good condition, that the tower was sound, that the mechanism turned freely, and that the light was not lit.

He was right.

And yet.

And yet, every night on the North Shelf, when the fog comes in over the water and the sea goes dark and the horizon goes empty, there is a moment — a moment that lasts about as long as it takes to turn your head — when the whole of the North Shelf seems to hold its breath.

The gulls stop their argument. The fog stops rolling. The sea stops moving. And for a moment, there is nothing at all.

And then, slowly, the gulls start again. The fog starts again. The sea starts again. And the horizon, which had been empty, is empty again.

It is the emptiest thing on the North Shelf. It is the emptiest thing anyone has ever seen. And it is the only thing that has ever been true.

The light was never the thing. The light was only the proof.

And the proof is gone.

And the North Shelf is, at last, completely, absolutely, and for the first time in a hundred and forty years, empty.

And that is the whole of it.

That is the whole of it. [end of text]


